What surprised me most about phishing attacks in 2026 is the speed and quality of AI-generated attacks. Gone are the "tell-tale" signs of poor grammar and poor phrasing. AI has now created perfect, highly personalized attacks that sound exactly like a trusted friend or a trusted entity, even mentioning projects you actually discussed
. Another surprise has been the evolution of phishing attacks to a "channel-hopping" attack, where a phishing attack begins on LinkedIn, moves to email, and ends with deepfake voice verification
. Hence, I will personally change my behavior around trusting in-app or messaging popups. Rather than relying on a Teams, Slack, or text-based popup, I will now adopt a "zero-trust" approach to any suspicious or unsolicited requests. Even if the sender is a trusted friend or a familiar entity, I will now verify any urgent requests, especially around money transfers or password changes, through a separate trusted communication channel before clicking on any links or sharing any data.
